Use 'scripts/repo-intel.lua' to navigate without reading whole source files:
- Run 'lua scripts/repo-intel.lua overview' before inspecting code.
- Use 'lua scripts/repo-intel.lua symbol <Name>' or 'deps <Name>' to inspect signatures and types.
- Run 'lua scripts/repo-intel.lua update' after saving any file.

- Analyze 'sales.csv' to identify best-selling categories and formulate new product/listing suggestions.
- I will specify whether to implement a new listing in Teal ('.tl') or C# ('.cs') with the corresponding DOM builder ('dom.tl' or Dom.cs).
- The 'tpr' watcher automatically compiles and renders PDFs upon save. It blocks forever, so never use it for a one-shot check: run 'scripts/render-check.sh' instead (single render + page-fit report in ~3 s, exit 1 on overflow).
- 'scripts/render-check.sh --style-append "li.item{line-height:1.16}"' probes a CSS tweak for one render and restores style.css automatically; '--images' also waits for the export PNGs; '--json' prints machine-readable results.

- Before writing a new listing or component, always inspect 1 or 2 existing templates in 'templates/' or documents/ to match structural conventions, naming styles, and DSL usage.
- Mirror the idioms used in the surrounding codebase (how records, data binding, and DOM elements are structured) so the generated code.
- After completing a new PDF listing, run the workflow defined in 'listing_prompt.txt' to generate the Etsy title, tags, and description.

- 'runtime.tl' is the entry point for the 'tpr' program.
- Use unique 2–4 word slug IDs for folder names, but customer-facing output filenames may collide or be shared across folders.
- Images should be enabled for final run.
